														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1504251">
									

<p>I downvoted for now. As is, the article is extremely abrupt and doesn't give much to explain itself. What was the object that impacted it? Surely it would at least have some research into it. Why would the Foundation simply look at it, think &quot;it's pulsating so it must be semiorganic, fuck it, nuke that thing?'</p>
<p>I think the core idea is sound, but it just needs more of a foundation to stand on. As is, it just jumps out, the article makes a few assumptions and then it's dead. What if it was broadcasting signals? What if it was infecting other satellites, and other instances remained in orbit to this day? What if the Starfish Prime nuke only disabled the electronic portions, but it self-repaired and is still up there, observing with no known motive?</p>
<p>It really needs a hook, otherwise it's just a dead, burnt lump in space. A wise man once said, regular lumps aren't scary, unless you're finding them on your balls.</p>

